<title>BELGIUM: W.Europe car registrations up 8.5 pct in Dec. [CORRECTED 11:50 GMT]</title>
<headline>W.Europe car registrations up 8.5 pct in Dec. [CORRECTED 11:50 GMT]</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1997-01-14</dateline>
<text>
<p>(Corrects percentage in headline from 6.5 pct).</p>
<p>The European Automobile Manufacturers Association (ACEA) released the following provisional West European new car registrations figures for December.</p>
<p> Dec 96			 Dec 95			pct change</p>
<p> 774,300			727,300		     +8.5 (Corrects from 6.5 pct)</p>
<p> Jan-Dec 96		   Jan-Dec 95</p>
<p> 12,803,700		   12,026,900		  +6.6 (corrects from 6.5 pct)</p>
<p>(Correction made after ACEA said a mistake had been made in calculations by official German government sources).</p>
<p>Following is a breakdown by manufacturer:</p>
<p>Brand		  1996	pct change   mkt share   vs   1995</p>
<p>							  (pct)	    (pct)</p>
<p>All brands     12,818,700	+6.6</p>
<p>Volkswagon	2,203,670	+9.3	   17.2	     16.8</p>
<p>GM		  1,602,222	+1.9	   12.5	     13.1</p>
<p>PSA		 1,528,534	+6.1	   11.9	     12.0</p>
<p>Ford		1,484,870	+4.2	   11.6	     11.9</p>
<p>Fiat		1,437,765	+7.6	   11.2	     11.1</p>
<p>Japanese	  1,371,405	+7.2	   10.7	     10.6</p>
<p>Renault	   1,289,677	+3.9	   10.1	     10.3</p>
<p>BMW		   804,907	+5.3	    6.3		6.4</p>
<p>Mercedes	    461,939     +14.2	    3.6		3.4</p>
<p>Korean		248,509     +38.0	    1.9		1.5</p>
<p>Volvo		 206,111	-5.5	    1.6		1.8</p>
<p>NOTE - Volkswagon includes Audi, SEAT, Skoda;</p>
<p>GM includes Opel/Vauxhall, U.S. imports, Saab, IBC/Isuzu;</p>
<p>Ford includes U.S. imports, Jaguar;</p>
<p>Fiat includes Lancia, Alfa romeo, Innocenti, Ferrari, Maserati;</p>
<p>Japanese includes Nissan, Toyota, Mazda, Honda, Mitsubishi and others;</p>
<p>BMW includes Rover; ACEA provides no breakdown for the Korean figures.</p>
<p>-- Brussels Newsroom +32 2 287 6810, Fax +32 2 230 7710</p>
</text>
